LOGICAL OPERATORS: 
The core logical operators in Java are AND (&&), OR (||), and NOT (!).
1. Logical AND (&&): Returns true only if both operands are true. If the first part is false, it stops checking.
2. Logical OR (||): Returns true if at least one operand is true. If the first part is true, it stops checking.
3. Logical NOT (!): Reverses the state of a boolean.(true becomes false, and false becomes true.)
4. Bitwise/Logical AND (&) and OR (|): Evaluate both sides regardless of the first operand's value.
                                       They do not short-circuit.(&& and || stop evaluating the expression as soon as the final result is known.)
Operator Precedence:
  ! has the highest precedence.
  && has higher precedence than ||.
  Always parentheses () need to be used to make complex conditions clear and to avoid bugs.


EXAMPLE PROGRAM:

import java.util.Scanner;
public class logicaloperators {
    public static void main(String args[])
    {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        
        System.out.print("Enter your percentage score (0-100): ");
        int score = input.nextInt();

        // Using OR (||) to catch errors first
        if (score < 0 || score > 100) {
            System.out.println("Error: Invalid score entered!");
        } 
        // Using AND (&&) to check ranges
        else if (score >= 90 && score <= 100) {
            System.out.println("EXCELLENT");
        } 
        else if (score >= 80 && score < 90) {
            System.out.println("GOOD");
        } 
        else if (score >= 70 && score < 80) {
            System.out.println("BETTER, CAN STILL IMPROVE");
        } 
        else {
            System.out.println("POOR, BETTER LUCK NEXT TIME");
        }

        input.close();
    }
}

NOTE: input.close(); tells the computer that your program is done reading data and can free up the system memory it was using.
        EXAMPLE:  hanging up a phone call when you are done talking so the line is free for others.
